%I A400630 #22 Oct 29 2026 00:06:42
%S A400630 2,8,14,22,30,40,50,62,74,88,102,118,134,152,170,190,210,232,254,278,
%T A400630 302,328,354,382,410,440,470,502,534,568,602,638,674,712,750,790,830,
%U A400630 872,914,958,1002,1048,1094,1142,1190,1240,1290,1342,1394,1448,1502,1558,1614
%N A400630 Number of semi-meanders with n crossings and winding number n-4, for n >= 4.
%C A400630 A semi-meander is a semi-infinite, self-avoiding, directed curve that crosses a line one or more times.
%C A400630 The winding number of a semi-meander is the hypothetical minimum number of self-crossings that would be created if a ray were to start at the source of the semi-infinite directed curve and extend to infinity without crossing the line.
%C A400630 The maximum winding number of a semi-meander with n crossings is n-2. Thus a(n) counts semi-meanders whose winding number is 2 less than the maximum.
%C A400630 Equivalent to a closed curve (a loop) transversely crossing a ray, considered up to continuous deformation fixing the ray, with a winding number equal to the number of crossings that would be created if the fixed end of the ray were extended to form a line.
%C A400630 Analogized to a river (a semi-meander, meander, or loop) crossing a road (a ray, line, or line segment).
%C A400630 The second diagonal of A400429, reading diagonals from the maximal-winding edge.
%H A400630 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400630/b400630.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 4..10000</a> (terms 4..300 from Hunter Hogan).
%H A400630 P. Di Francesco, O. Golinelli, and E. Guitter, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9607039">Meanders: A Direct Enumeration Approach</a>, arXiv:hep-th/9607039, 1996.
%H A400630 Hunter Hogan, <a href="https://github.com/hunterhogan/mapFolding">mapFolding: Create, alter, and optimize algorithms to count meanders, stamp foldings, and map foldings</a>, GitHub repository, 2026.
%H A400630 The Omega Institute, <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/blob/5c0f5b1b3f3b07e72bf5ba6cc8b7daf4b2cc428d/D5/S3/Combinatorics/SemiMeanderSecondDiagonal.lean">Lean 4 formal proof of the connected semi-meander second-diagonal formula</a>, TruReTuring, GitHub repository, 2026.
%H A400630 Joe Sawada and Roy Li, <a href="https://doi.org/10.37236/2404">Stamp Foldings, Semi-Meanders, and Open Meanders: Fast Generation Algorithms</a>, The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, Volume 19 No. 2 (2012), #P43 (16 pages).
%H A400630 <a href="/index/Rec#order_04">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (2,0,-2,1).
%F A400630 a(n) = T(n,floor(n/2)-1), where T is the triangle A400429.
%F A400630 More generally, if D_j is the j-th diagonal of A400429, then 2*j is its least index.
%F A400630 For this sequence, j = 2 and 2*j = 4.
%F A400630 D_j(2*j) = a(4) = A005315(j) = A005316(2*j-1).
%F A400630 D_j(2*j+1) = a(5) = A005315(j+1) = A005316(2*j+1) = D_{j+1}(2*j+2) = A400631(6).
%F A400630 a(n) = (n^2 + 2*n + (n mod 2) - 20)/2, for n >= 4.
%F A400630 a(n) = floor((n+1)^2/2) - 10.
%F A400630 a(n) = a(n-2) + 2*n, for n >= 6, with a(4) = 2 and a(5) = 8.
%F A400630 From _Elmo R. Oliveira_, Oct 28 2026: (Start)
%F A400630 G.f.: 2*x^4*(1 + 2*x - x^2 - x^3) / ((1 - x)^3*(1 + x)).
%F A400630 E.g.f.: 10 + 8*x + 3*x^2 + x^3/3 + ((x^2 + 3*x - 20)*cosh(x) + (x^2 + 3*x - 19)*sinh(x))/2.
%F A400630 a(n) = (2*n^2 + 4*n - 39 - (-1)^n)/4.
%F A400630 a(n) = 2*a(n-1) - 2*a(n-3) + a(n-4). (End)
%e A400630 Row n=10 of A400429 is 262,752,340,50,2, corresponding to winding numbers 0,2,4,6,8. Since n-4=6, a(10)=50, which is the second-to-last value.
%t A400630 Quotient[Range[5, 60]^2, 2] - 10 (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 11 2026 *)
%Y A400630 Second diagonal of A400429.
%Y A400630 Cf. A005315, A005316, A400631, A400632, A400633, A400634, A400635, A400636, A400637, A400638, A400639.
